// A `friend` grant on a contract grants nothing: a contract is a PUBLIC interface, so it has no private
// member to open — every method satisfying one must be `public`. It used to PARSE and be silently dropped
// (`collectClasses` reads friend grants, `collectInterfaces` never did), so a non-granted caller still
// reached the member and a grant naming a function that does not exist was accepted — while the same typo
// on a TYPE is caught by `friend_unknown`. Accepted-and-inert surface that reads as protection.
type contract Tok for resource {
    fn void begin();
    friend driver[begin];
}
type resource Impl implements Tok {
    public ctor make() { }
    public fn void begin() { }
}
fn void driver(ref Tok t) { t.begin(); }
fn int32 main() { Impl i = Impl.make(); return 0; }
